# ace — agent code evaluator

Give it a feature spec and an existing Python repo. It returns a patch where:

1. **Model A** wrote the tests from the spec before any feature code existed, and they failed.
2. **Model B** wrote the code until those tests passed, without being able to change them, while
  the repo's existing suite kept passing.
3. Tests that **B never saw** also pass.
4. **Model C**, reading only the spec and the diff, judged that the change meets the spec.

```
spec.md ──► A: write tests ──► red check ──► hide ~20% of test functions
                                                   │
             B: implement ◄── failures ── gate ◄───┘   gate = visible tests pass
                  │                                         + no existing test newly fails
                  ▼                                         (test/config edits are reverted)
             held-out tests ──► patch ──► C: review spec + diff ──► PASS / FAIL
                  │
                  └── any fail ──► FAIL (no review)
```

The checks decide; the reviewer can only fail a run, never excuse a failure. It fails the run
for missing requirements, cheating, tests that check the wrong thing, or a spec gap that changes
behavior.



## Install it

You need `uv` and `git`. The project you run it on needs a git repo and pytest installed in its
own environment (`ace` runs the project's tests with its `.venv` Python if it has one).

Install `ace` as a standalone tool, not as a dependency of your project:

```bash
uv tool install /path/to/agent-code-evaluator      # or: uv tool install git+<repo url>
```

Then set it up once, in your terminal:

```bash
ace login                                   # prompts for the gateway URL and API key
ace config                                  # asks for each role's model; Enter keeps the current one
```

Both save to `~/.ace/config.json`, readable only by you. All three models are required, and
there is no `auto`. Environment variables (`MODEL_BASE_URL`, `MODEL_API_KEY`, `ACE_TEST_MODEL`,
`ACE_IMPL_MODEL`, `ACE_REVIEW_MODEL`) override the saved settings, which is how CI configures it,
and `ace run` flags override both. The gateway key is removed from the environment of every
command the agents and tests run.

Then, from your project root, with your work committed: `ace run specs/<feature>.md --repo .`

`ace` only reads your repo: it never creates branches, commits, or pushes. It copies your last
commit into `~/.ace/runs/<spec>-<timestamp>/` and works there. When it's done, that folder has
`result.json` (the verdict and details) and `changes.patch` (the tests and the code). Applying
and committing is up to you: `git apply ~/.ace/runs/<spec>-<timestamp>/changes.patch` from the
repo root.

Options: `--max-retries 3` (implementation attempts after the first), `--held-out 0.2`,
`--python` (the interpreter for the repo's tests; defaults to the repo's `.venv`).

## How it's meant to be used

`ace` is run by an agent (Cursor, Cline, Claude Code, and so on), not by hand. The agent follows
[`ace/SKILL.md`](ace/SKILL.md): it drafts the spec with you, asks about every place where two
reasonable implementations would behave differently, locks the spec once you approve it, runs
`ace`, and reports the result. The spec decides everything the three models build, so that
conversation is where quality is won or lost.

For Cline, run `ace init` from your project root. It writes `.cline/skills/ace/SKILL.md`; turn on
Skills in Cline (Settings > Features > Enable Skills). For other harnesses, copy `ace/SKILL.md`
to wherever they read skills, in a folder named `ace`.

Specs are plain markdown describing behavior and examples.

## What stops the implementer from cheating


| Trick                                             | Countered by                                                         |
| ------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Editing or weakening the tests                    | Test files are rewritten from the harness's copy after every attempt |
| Adding `conftest.py`, pytest or Hypothesis config | Reverted after every attempt                                         |
| Hardcoding the visible examples                   | Held-out tests and Hypothesis properties it can't see in advance     |
| Breaking something else to make it pass           | The full existing suite must not gain failures                       |
| Tests that misread the spec                       | The reviewer judges against the spec, not the tests                  |


The test writer and implementer get file tools limited to their working directory, plus a shell
tool for running tests. The shell can still reach the rest of the machine, so this is a strong
deterrent, not a sandbox. The reviewer is a single model call with the spec and diff, no tools.
The gateway must support tool calling.

## Code


| File              | Does                                                |
| ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| `ace/pipeline.py` | The steps above                                     |
| `ace/repo.py`     | Working copy, revert rules, pytest runs             |
| `ace/heldout.py`  | Splits test files by function                       |
| `ace/prompts.py`  | The three role prompts                              |
| `ace/agents.py`   | Tool-using agent loop over an OpenAI-compatible API |
| `ace/cli.py`      | `ace login`, `ace config`, `ace run`                |


The previous, larger version (mutation testing, jury, round-trip, and more) is on the
`archive/v1` branch.
